\subsection{Cross-Architecture NLI Verification}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Motivation.}
The oracle gap (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) demonstrates that the MAX-SAT aggregation mechanism is sound: given gold constraints, SICA achieves $+14.22$\,pp over SC. The bottleneck is self-extraction quality, and twenty-five same-model remediation strategies fail to improve it (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). This implies that the corrective signal must come from outside the reasoning model's own distribution.

\paragraph{Method.}
We test this hypothesis with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M parameters, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, as described in \S\ref{sec:cross-arch-method}. For each problem, DeBERTa classifies the (premise, conclusion) pair into \textsc{entailment}, \textsc{contradiction}, or \textsc{neutral}. In combo mode, the NLI verdict is integrated with SC's $K$ votes via a weight parameter $w$: each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\%, below SC's 54.41\% baseline. Combo mode at $w{=}1$ (NLI as 13th voter) reaches 57.84\% ($\Delta = +3.43$\,pp, McNemar exact $p = 0.039$; discordant pairs: $b{=}1$, $c{=}8$ of 9 total; 95\% bootstrap CI $[0.98, 6.37]$\,pp). The small discordant count ($n{=}9$) warrants caution, though the asymmetry ($c/b = 8$) is strongly directional. This is the only same-dataset result in our experiments that reaches $p < 0.05$ with a positive effect. Weight sensitivity is monotonic: $w{=}3$ gives $+4.41$\,pp ($p = 0.078$, not significant; $b{=}6$, $c{=}15$), $w{=}5$ gives $+5.88$\,pp (best absolute, 60.29\%; no independent significance test). We report $w{=}1$ as the primary result because it is the only configuration with a significant McNemar test.

\paragraph{Per-label complementarity.}
The combo gain is not uniform across labels. On \textsc{False} problems, NLI achieves 71.43\% compared to SC's 38.10\% ($+33$\,pp), because DeBERTa's contradiction detection directly identifies negated conclusions that the LLM's chain-of-thought reasoning systematically mishandles. On \textsc{Unknown} problems, the pattern reverses: SC reaches 68.12\% while NLI drops to 18.84\% ($+49$\,pp for SC), because ``neutral'' in NLI does not map cleanly to the logical \textsc{Unknown} label. The combo succeeds because these strengths are complementary. When both systems agree (45.1\% of problems), accuracy reaches 68.48\% ($+14.07$\,pp over SC), confirming that agreement between architecturally independent systems serves as a high-precision signal.

\paragraph{Why cross-architecture verification works.}
The 406M encoder-only model and the 7B decoder-only LLM share no architecture, training data, or inference mechanism. DeBERTa classifies textual entailment rather than generating chain-of-thought, so it does not exhibit the symbolic confirmation bias identified in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}. The two systems err on different problem subsets, satisfying the independence condition that same-model sampling cannot provide. This validates the causal model: because confirmation bias originates from the shared distribution of a single model, external signal with an independent error profile breaks the cycle.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Self-Consistency and the Condorcet Independence Assumption}
\label{sec:condorcet}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. The Condorcet Jury Theorem (CJT) \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees that if $K$ independent voters are each correct with probability $p > 0.5$, the majority converges to truth as $K \to \infty$ \citep{austensmith1996}. LLM traces violate this independence: all $K$ samples come from the same model with only temperature as variation. Following \citet{ladha1992correlated} and the design-effect framework of \citet{kish1965survey}, we define effective sample size using Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa} as proxy for intraclass correlation:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = \frac{K}{1 + (K - 1) \cdot \kappa}.
\end{equation}
In our experiments (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82 across instruction-tuned pairs (0.91 for thinking mode), yielding $K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.2\text{--}3.6$ for $K = 12$. This \emph{independence bottleneck} motivates our investigation.

\subsection{SICA Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts FOL constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}). The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence; each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout. Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score, making SICA a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration condition.} If self-extracted constraints are coupled to their source answer, MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. We show in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias} that this degeneration occurs in practice.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0$ (fraction of questions with $>1$ distinct answer) is a zero-cost diagnostic computable before extraction. When $C_0 < 5\%$, SICA produces the same output as SC.
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:c0-def}
C_0 = \frac{|\{q \in \mathcal{Q} : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}|}{|\mathcal{Q}|}.
\end{equation}

